 # Hosted correctness, native vector correctness and Zen 5 benchmarks
 
+## Every update to main
+
+Every push to `main`, including a documentation/Markdown-only PR merge or a
+direct push, starts `9700x.yml` against that push's exact `github.sha` (#1271).
+Merge, squash and rebase merges use this same push trigger. Main has no path
+filter and no no-code exemption: it runs both hosted architectures, all four
+shards of every existing plain/sanitizer row (including the additional O1
+address/undefined checks), and the existing fuzz campaigns. A failed setup or
+test remains a failure, not a substitute for completed coverage.
+
+Each main push has a run-specific concurrency group. Later merges therefore
+cannot replace an older pending main run; `cancel-in-progress: false` in one
+shared group would not prevent that replacement. PR runs still share their
+PR's group and cancel superseded revisions. The host's existing lock still
+serializes hardware jobs without serializing the full hosted workflows.
+
+Nightly mode and successful PR native evidence never replace the post-merge
+hosted suite: the push evidence decision remains `scope=full`, `hosted=run`.
+Native-evidence reuse and nightly measurement selection retain their separate
+policies below; full hosted CI does not mean every nightly benchmark runs
+after every merge. The no-code exemption applies only to pre-merge PR checks.
+
+The selector's `--self-test` checks the unfiltered main trigger, independent
+push groups, full hosted gates/matrix and exact-revision fuzz checkout, with
+mutants for lost coverage. It also tests all file classes in both push
+selection modes. `tests/ci/nightly.sh` checks the real evidence glue on merged,
+documentation-only and direct-push fixtures, with nightly mode on/off and
+native reuse; `qualification --self-test` rejects an all-skipped main push.
+
+GitHub platform restrictions still apply: Actions must be enabled, and an
+automation must use a credential/event route that emits workflow events.
+Pushes made using a workflow's `GITHUB_TOKEN` do not automatically start other
+push workflows. Such merge automation must use an approved GitHub App or
+personal access token, or explicitly arrange an exact-revision run; a
+storage-policy check or a green PR check alone does not prove main coverage.
+Do not use commit-message skip directives when merging: they suppress push
+workflows before this policy can execute. See GitHub's
+[triggering rules](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/write-workflows/choose-when-workflows-run/trigger-a-workflow),
+[skip directives](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/manage-workflow-runs/skip-workflow-runs)
+and [concurrency behavior](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/concepts/workflows-and-actions/concurrency).
+
